\section{Missingness lives in \code{family}}{


R reserves the bit pattern \code{0x80000000} as \code{NA_integer_}, so a word cannot
use \code{NA} to mean "absent" without also losing the one address that has that
pattern. raddr therefore reads a word as raw bits and nothing else:
\code{NA_integer_} in \code{w1}-\code{w4} means the pattern \code{0x80000000}, not missingness.

A row is a missing address if and only if its \code{family} is \code{NA}, and \code{is.na()}
reports exactly that.

The consequence a caller can see is that \verb{128.0.0.0} compares equal to
itself, which is not true of every R package that stores addresses this way.
}

\section{Equality and ordering}{


Equality is over the 128 bits and the family, and nothing else. The \strong{zone
does not participate}: \verb{fe80::1\%lo0} equals \verb{fe80::1\%en0}, because they are
the same address named on two interfaces. Query \code{\link[=addr_zone]{addr_zone()}} when the
interface matters.

Ordering is \strong{total}, so \code{\link[=sort]{sort()}} and \code{\link[=order]{order()}} work on a vector mixing
families: IPv4 sorts before IPv6, and the 4-in-6 form sorts with IPv6 by its
full 128 bits. Ordering agrees with equality -- \code{x == y} implies
\code{vctrs::vec_compare(x, y)} is \code{0}.
}

